<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athelney</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athelney is a low hill rising above the marshes of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a> Levels in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfred-the-great">Alfred the Great</a> took refuge there after the Danish attack on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chippenham">Chippenham</a> in January 878, building a fort from which he harassed the enemy before his victory at Edington. He later founded a monastery at Athelney in thanksgiving, and the Alfred Jewel was found nearby in 1693.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athelney.jpg" alt="Athelney" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Richard Webb · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:River_Tone,_Athelney_-_geograph.org.uk_-_4437250.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Athelney lies between the villages of Burrowbridge and East Lyng in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>. It is known as the Isle of Athelney because it was once a low, isolated island in the swampy and impassable marshes of the Somerset Levels, much of which lie below the level of high tide. The Levels are now drained for farming in summer but are regularly flooded in winter. The name is thought to come from the Old English æþeling, prince, and -ey, isle, so that Æthelinga īeg may mean the Island of Princes, which might point to royal links before Alfred.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations and written evidence show that in Alfred's time the island was joined to East Lyng by a causeway, the Balt Moor Wall, with a semi-circular stockade and ditch at either end. The ditch on the island dates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>, so Alfred probably knew the place as an old fort and strengthened its defences. Traces of metalworking suggest that he also used it to equip his army before defeating the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-heathen-army">Great Heathen Army</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-edington">Battle of Edington</a> in May 878.</p><p>In thanks for that victory Alfred founded Athelney Abbey on the isle in 888. It lasted until the Dissolution of the Monasteries under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-viii-of-england">Henry VIII</a> in 1539, when the value of its rubble was put at £80, and the monks afterwards built the church of East Lyng. Nothing of the abbey stands above ground. Excavations for the television programme Time Team in 1993, and again in its 100th episode a decade later, showed it to be one of the few known <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> metalworking sites in the UK.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>A small monument built in 1801 by Sir John Slade, 1st Baronet, on the site of a stone vault marks where the abbey stood. It is a Scheduled Ancient Monument and a Grade II listed building, on the land of Athelney Farm, reached by a permissive path. North Petherton, where the Alfred Jewel was found, lies about 6 miles away.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Athelney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In January 878 a surprise Danish attack on Chippenham drove Alfred into the marshes of <b>Athelney</b>, but in May he defeated Guthrum at the Battle of Edington.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wessex">Kingdom of Wessex · Military</a></li></ul></section>
